rsonal  responsibility for the by-election  defeat in north dropship was the right  note to take and i think he's going to  have to take personal responsibility for  the investigation into these parties as  well  polly what do you think  i wasn't enormously impressed by the  prime minister's apology today in that  kind of extraordinary interview with  your correspondent sam coates  actually the prime minister said  uh he takes full responsibility for  everything that happens in the  government because he and the government  didn't persuade the journalists not to  focus on parties  and that's an odd kind of way of taking  responsibility for him it seems to be  all about a kind of media management  strategy  and for me it's the fundamental thing  there is an absolutely cast iron  guaranteed way to stop the media talking  about your bad behavior and it's not to  do the bad behavior  unfortunately there's no way to go back  in time and  deal with this issue  but you know it that's why it's so  difficult to deal with because it really  does speak to the character  of both the prime minister and his  leadership and the staff and the people  he puts around him i mean it's  completely absurd to have put simon case  in charge of this investigation into  what parties happened when he himself in  his office seems to have hosted or  enabled to go ahead a party which may or  may not have been a breach of the rules  we don't know but certainly is at the  margin of what is or isn't allowed and  it's interesting because some of the  commentary around this seems to be  questioning whether it was reasonable or  not to have those rules in place and  whether it should have been all right to  have a party gathering after work  amongst colleagues who were already  spending the day together and that's a  reasonable debate but it's a reasonable  debate for us to have had last year  those were the rules in place it was  very clear you should not have a work  party or social gathering and so for the  people who put those rules in place to  have broken them is extremely serious  the person who's going to take over this  investigation from simon case is sue  gray who is forensic and formidable used  to be uh the director general in charge  of propriety and ethics now second  permanent secretary at the housing uh  and leveling up department and you know  i think we can have quite a lot of faith  in her but the problem is that the prime  minister that's why i do agree with alex  the prime minister is going to have to  accept that he permitted either a  culture or that tolerated that level of  wrongdoing or that he directly permitted  those parties and saying that you take  responsibility for that it's about more  than just blaming the media for  reporting about it alex  hang on i i agree with some of what  polly says i of course agree with the  praise for sue gray and i think that  she's the right person to take over but  the simon case was definitely the right  person to appoint because his cabinet  secretary the issue for me now on these  stories is twofold first of all if simon  case was at a party that was uh alleged  to have been in breach of these things  he surely would have known that and he  should have recused himself at the  beginning rather than now i can't  believe that people are sitting around  waiting for somebody to say hang on  weren't you at one of these before they  say oh okay i've got to step back from  it but the second point is that no one  seems to have argued from the government  perspective including those in the last  week myself included who've been  defending boris and the prime minister  that london as i recall was in tier two  at the time which permitted work  gatherings and it seems to me that that  seems to be the way out for a lot of the  discussion it doesn't look good but that  was within the rules as far as i recall  yeah the times now looking at the uh  future investigation and polly the  question has to be  whether the public will have uh any  confidence in in future investigations  into these parties  i think it's quite hard for people to  have confidence partly because it's  genuinely complicated um  there's a lot of different parties  there are i believe there's supposed  parties that may have occurred in the  downing street flat there's parties that  occurred uh within downing street within  the office area there's potentially  simon casey's party there's allegations  that there were parties uh at the  department for work and pensions and i  i've lost track and i'm supposed to keep  up with this stuff so  for an investigation to  go through all of those in a systematic  way to collect all of the evidence  um  you know in a in a patient and  considered way  will is necessary but even then it's  just it's quite hard to get over that  hump of public opinion which comes out  of the sense of chaos and confusion that  has reigned over all of this and that is  public that is public opinion that's  being reflected within the conservative  party the ft uh reporting uh about angry  conservatives warning the prime minister  he could be out by mid 2022. alex do you  think that might be the case  i don't actually i don't think that the  letters are going to be flying into sir  graham brady but i do  think that polly was right about that  sense of of confusion and  i think that it played a significant  part in the north shore by-election  results i thought the front of the times  captured these things well um the the  person appointed to oversee an inquiry  into these parties the one that polly  forgot was the one at cchq allegedly and  the one that led to sean bailey stepping  down uh from one of his roles um in  london uh the the the case that's been  made on the front of the times um is  that you appointed this person to  oversee things and now that person's  having to accuse himself well that's  right because for the last week we've  been pointing towards simon case as a  figure of authority who can oversee  these inquiries and number 10 must have  known or at least simon case himself  must have known that he couldn't with  authority oversee that given that he was  in attendance allegedly at one of these  parties himself so it's very tricky but  no i don't think actually that these  letters will be going into uh  graham brady not least because a large  number of conservative mps owe their  seats to boris johnson he after all won  the 80-seat majority that the party now  enjoys in parliament so government will  now be wishing desperately just to get  to christmas have a fire break between  all these events in 2022 and have a  reset next year  polly very quickly can the prime  minister keep the support of  his party the daily express saying doubt  but not out why we must put faith in  boris  yeah they're quoting jacob bruce mogg  who's kind of trying to rally the troops  i think alex is right it's unlikely that  this will lead to uh to direct the  resignation of boris johnson or or to  him being ousted by his mps but i do  think it's potentially the the beginning  of the end for for his popularity with  those people and if covet continues to  get worse if inflation continues to get  worse if there isn't kind of light at  the end of the tunnel in terms of the  government actually achieving things  that pressure will worsen let's continue  with the eye shall we start with that  one this section uh and that  front page looking at uh the infections  rates of omicron um  doubling every one and a half days in  london and northeast of england alex  well it depends on what you think out of  control is it's definitely true that  omicron is more virulent and that london  as the least jabbed area of the country  is experiencing this worse than some  other parts but it does seem that the  assertions that we received when we went  through our vaccination program that  vaccines were going to  break the connection between infection  and hospitalization still remains  broadly true  so we are having many more cases  reported as the front of the eye says  but given that we are testing at twice  the rate we were testing last week or a  year ago if you make the annual  comparison that's not that surprising  the important thing will be whether  we're seeing an uptick in  hospitalizations or sadly in deaths and  so far at least  what would you say out of control would  be how would you measure that  well that's my point if the only  consequence of people being infected  with omicron is that they get infected  then that actually given that we're all  going to have coronavirus sooner or  later doesn't matter too much the thing  that matters to our country is whether  people have to go to hospital or whether  we would get seriously ill and if we've  broken the connection between infection  and hospitalization then actually people  getting coronavirus given we're all  going to have it isn't the main thing is  it  polly do you agree with that  i do it's just that the numbers now for  omicron appear to be getting so large  that even though we have the vaccines in  place and have been doing a phenomenal  work uh in terms of the booster program  that there is a real danger that those  hospitalizations which are starting to  creep up especially in london deaths  which aren't yet but we know that that  is a a lagging indicator it takes time  for people to go from catching the  disease to getting very yell through to  getting into hospital through to losing  their life so we know that takes longer  it just what the  medical scientists are telling us is  that those numbers are starting to look  really very worrying and of course it's  often we hear this sense that oh the nhs  is obsessing about covid and it's  forgotten about heart disease and cancer  and strokes and diabetes  actually it's  it's that if omicron gets out of control  if we have those hospitalizations from  covid that eats up capacity of the nhs  and reduces its ability to think about  cancer and heart disease and  and  it's wider services to the community so  i think you know there just there is  this risk now that we are going to see  numbers which are beyond what the nhs  can manage easily very quickly i just  want to bring alex in on the daily mail  front page and this cutting of the the  isolation days that's been suggested  very quickly we've only got about 20  seconds sorry  suggestion that we should reduce the  isolation required if you are um pinged  or if you're if you're sick and i think  that must be right given that in the end  we've got rid of our red list most of us  are going to get coronavirus sooner or  later we've got to get back to normal  and some of that's got to be reflected  in a reduction in isolation times

